          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you (in this context: I love you as a sister loves a brother).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Jane explicitly presents her sisterly affection for St. John as true. The qualification makes this a clear avowal of familial or friendly love rather than romantic love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The love expression itself undertakes no marriage, missionary service, or future conduct. Jane expressly denies being obligated to go to India. Her separate statements about serving as St. John’s assistant are not part of the undertaking force of “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with strangers.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a reasoned explanation distinguishing sisterly affection from marital commitment. Nothing supplied represents it as a reflex-like or uncontrolled affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression defines and limits the relationship: Jane categorizes her love as sisterly, thereby rejecting St. John’s attempted framing of them as prospective spouses.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane addresses St. John during a dispute over his proposal that she marry him and accompany him to India. She explains that her willingness to venture with him arose from admiration, trust, and sisterly love, while firmly denying romantic or marital commitment.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no undertaking. Separately, Jane had offered to accompany St. John as an assistant or sister, but refused marriage; St. John rejected that arrangement as unsuitable and proposed that she assist a different, married missionary. Jane then denied that she had any binding promise or obligation to go to India. Her characterization of the love as sisterly receives no affirmative uptake from St. John.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre. This classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence clearly establishes the expression’s sisterly, non-romantic framing and its lack of promissory force. It does not independently settle every nuance of Jane’s broader feelings or St. John’s private response beyond what the supplied narration represents.\"}"
